  "details": "I don't feel that this should have to be said, but I really, REALLY feel that this is a feature that should 100% be a thing. (Screenshots provided below.)\n\nI genuinely feel that there should be an option to be able to revert to previous versions of the VRChat UI. What do I mean? Well, let me give some examples.\n\nBefore the \"Main Menu 2.0\" Update which updated the quick menu, the Quick Menu for a long time was the classic, box-styled menu with an extremely cool looking blue-ball for a cursor that just looked really, really cool. This UI was known all across VRChat. It was clean, easy-to-read and navigate, and overall felt very belonged in the game. It was extremely unique to VRChat and I feel that players should 100% have the option to use the old UI if they so choose that they want to. \n\nSame concept with the updated \"Big Menu\" (aka the menu that is brought up once you click on any main tab on the Quick Menu). The ability to switch between the new and the old versions of UI elements/versions would be so amazing as not only a nod to previous elements of VRChat that were so loved by players, but would give a sense of freedom to the playerbase and give players more control over how their experience looks, feels, and plays. \n\nA third example I would love to bring up is the nameplate update. The Post-Updated nameplates from 2017-2019, although most known during the 2018 era, were so indistinguishable and iconic. They were incredibly clean, easy-to-read, and were totally unique to VRChat and no other game. They even had clear indicators on the nameplates themselves like showing a persons rank, whether they're speaking or not, and their avatar rank all on one nameplate. It was quick, informative, and gave unique, yet easy-to-understand ways of dishing out surface level information to the player. Their colors even made them truly \"pop\" to players.\n\nAnd finally, the last UI example I want to bring up: The 2018-19 loading screen. The loading screen where it had looked like you were quite literally travelling through space and time itself as beautiful, ambient music played. I cannot even describe how cool this was to me and so many others. It made something as simple as travelling to other worlds in VRChat as not just going to another lobby, but literally taking a journey through a \"portal\" into another world. The small things really, really made VRChat special, and this was one of those things. \n\nAn honorable mention I'd love to add is the UI sound effects. The new UI sound effects more recently just sound bland. Generic. Basic. However, the previous sounds that were used before the UI updates, specifically when you clicked on buttons, sounded almost sci-fi, further solidifying the idea that VRChat is this sort of \"out of this world\" experience. As I said before, the small things made a huge difference.\n\nWhile the improvements made to VRChat's overall UI and the features that have been added are insanely good, I do feel that if we as players had the option to switch to the older UI versions/UI elements that had compatibility with the newly added features, it would just be an incredibly appreciated addition all around the playerbase and add another in-depth layer of detailed player-to-player experience and customization. \n\nVRChat developers, if you're reading this, I know the thing you're wanting to say right now is \"these changes were made because of flaws with the previous UI and etc.\" While that is understood, the old UI elements and functions at their core were still well-known and loved by all players and simply updating their flaws and compatibility and adding the option to change to those versions of the UI would be an incredibly welcomed addition by both old and new players of VRChat. \n\nChange is good, VRChat. But, sometimes the best things are worth keeping and preserving. \n\nThank you for reading.",
